# --- fielded/ : the fielded shell's build products, preserved -----------------
# fielded/<static_id>/ holds the ONLY inputs from which a new overlay can ever
# be added to a shell that is on a board -- above all static_routed_locked.dcp,
# which `make add-rm-* BUILD=<tree>` reads (fpga/dfx/build_dfx.tcl incremental
# mode). Until 2026-09-09 exactly one copy existed, in a gitignored Vivado
# scratch tree; a `make clean` would have closed the fielded shell permanently.
#
# TRACKED here: README.md, MANIFEST.md5, fetch_fielded.sh, verify_fielded.sh --
# the provenance, the checksums, and the two scripts that repopulate and check
# the directory. Those are small and are the point. From 0x3F1A560F also
# mint.json, static_id.txt, overlay_inputs.txt and static_stamp.json: that mint
# WROTE a record rather than needing one reconstructed, and the record is text.
#
# NOT tracked: the ~50 MB of binaries themselves (already matched by the global
# *.dcp / *.bit / *.xsa rules above; named again here so the intent is explicit
# rather than incidental). git-LFS vs an external store was DECIDED on
# 2026-09-10 in favour of the external store -- see the "Tracking these
# binaries" section of fielded/0xA8C1C535/README.md for the full argument, which
# fielded/0x3F1A560F/README.md carries forward rather than restating. So the
# binaries live in local scratch + the hub, and MANIFEST.md5 is what proves a
# recovered copy is the right one.
fielded/*/*.dcp
fielded/*/*.xsa
fielded/*/*.bit
# The Linux records' fetched binaries (CUTOVER-PREP, 2026-09-29): the slot images and the
# ~200 MB Buildroot legal-info tar are listed in MANIFEST.md5 and fetched, never tracked.
fielded/*/*.img
fielded/*/*.tar

# fpga/dfx/tools/ is deliberately NOT ignored: the `fpga/dfx/build*/` rule above
# matches build-prefixed dirs only, and these five helpers are tracked source
# (they used to live inside build_clcd/, where clone could not reach them).

# tools.env -- site-local tool paths, source-tree locations and MINT_HUB.
# The tracked template is tools.env.example. A real tools.env names one host's
# filesystem and must never be committed (fpga/dfx/Makefile -includes it).
/tools.env
